WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.747 --> 00:00:12.080
AMEER ABBAS: Hello, and welcome.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.120
My name is Ameer Abbas.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.100
I'm a product manager at Google.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.000
I focus on application and
platform modernization.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.650
Last year I delivered
a presentation

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.690
with an eerily similar
topic to this one.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.670
It was called "Building
Globally Scalable

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.220
Applications with Istio
and Anthos Service Mesh."

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.140
So you're probably
wondering why am I doing

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.430
the same presentation again.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.247
Well, not quite.

00:00:31.247 --> 00:00:33.580
I thought this year I would
take some of the foundations

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.740
and principles we
talked about last year

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.870
and put them into practice,
and I can show you

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.330
some of the practical
applications of delivering

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.750
modern applications.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.190
So let's get started.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.350
Let's talk about a service.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.150
Everything revolves
around a service.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.610
We build our teams, our
organizations, our engineering,

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:51.640
our product teams to deliver
beautiful, scalable, resilient

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.200
services to our customers.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.933
Every service
needs a few things.

00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:56.850
For instance, a service
needs to be resilient.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.700
It needs to be scalable.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.010
It needs to be secure.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.200
And most importantly, it
needs to be manageable.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.569
And what we mean by
that is by resiliency

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.340
I mean that we need
to be able to weather

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.950
the storms against any
infrastructure, human,

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.840
or machine error.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.610
Scalability is pretty
self-explanatory.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.040
We need to be able to run our
services on many computers,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.750
maybe in many
Kubernetes clusters

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.450
in a lot of different
zones in many regions.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.990
For security, we want to encrypt
all traffic, all communication

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:24.100
between services.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.500
And we want to be able to
have the tools to implement

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.880
policy and enforce policy to
and from every single service.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.590
And for manageability,
there's a lot

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.570
of things that fall
under that umbrella.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.940
For instance, what do
we want to observe?

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:38.740
How do we want to
measure the signals?

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.710
How do we deliver traffic to
the right instance of a service?

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.500
And how do we reliably roll
out new versions of a service

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.810
and keep innovating and
manage the configuration that

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.040
revolves around a service?

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.660
All of that falls
under manageability.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:51.770
So let's recap.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.540
Last year we talked
about running services

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:54.710
in a single cluster.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.480
Here we have a service
called front end

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.580
running in the front end
namespace with a pod.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.290
And it's running in
a single cluster.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:03.500
And everything is fine and dandy
until that cluster goes down.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.120
And as soon as that
cluster goes down,

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.740
that service and
any other service

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.240
that's running in that cluster
also goes down with it.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.360
So we've kind of
broken that first tenet

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.630
of resiliency, which
is that we can't even

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.100
weather the storms against
infrastructure failures.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.270
So what do we do?

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:21.060
Well, we run our services
in more than one cluster.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.510
So we run something called
distributed services.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.820
A distributed service is
just a Kubernetes service

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.830
that runs on multiple clusters.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:31.160
From an outsider's perspective,
it's the same logical service.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.350
I don't need to know that it's
running on multiple clusters.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.110
So here I have the
same service front

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:37.670
end running on four clusters.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.530
On the left-hand side,
I have two clusters

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.563
in region 1 in two
different zones.

00:02:41.563 --> 00:02:42.980
And on the right-hand
side, I have

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.540
two clusters in region 2
in two different zones.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.350
So you can see that not only
do I have cluster resiliency,

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:51.320
I have zonal redundancy
and regional redundancy.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.660
And what that means is
if my cluster goes down,

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:54.997
I still haven't lost my service.

00:02:54.997 --> 00:02:57.080
I have three other clusters
that can serve traffic

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.200
for that service.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.580
I can lose an entire region and
I have not lost that service.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.730
I can lose multiple clusters
in multiple regions.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:06.860
As long as that last
cluster has enough capacity

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.080
to serve traffic for
that particular service,

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:10.110
I'm good to go.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.600
So you can already
see the effects

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.130
of scalability and
resiliency when

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.200
you run distributed services.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.600
But with distributed services
comes a few considerations

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.210
that you may not consider
in a single cluster life.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:22.850
For example, service
discovery and traffic routing.

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:25.430
In a single cluster, service
discovery and traffic routing

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:26.505
are kind of automatic.

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:28.130
When you drop a
service into a cluster,

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:29.600
it gets automatically
discovered.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.010
Other services can get to it.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.405
Routing just works.

00:03:32.405 --> 00:03:34.280
So how do we do that in
a distributed service

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.300
environment?

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.370
Here we have two
distributed services.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:38.900
What if the front
end service wants

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.490
to talk to the cart service?

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.020
How does the front
end service know

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.480
which pods are healthy
for the cart service?

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.450
Which pods to get to and
how to route to those pods.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.750
So this is kind of the
east-west traffic problem.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.920
Next is ingress.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.540
Again in a single
cluster environment,

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:54.980
we have an ingress object.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:56.808
We deploy the ingress
object, and we

00:03:56.808 --> 00:03:59.225
can get traffic from outside
of the cluster to our service

00:03:59.225 --> 00:04:00.020
no problem.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.270
Well, how do we do that in
a multicluster environment?

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.460
How do we do a
multicluster ingress?

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:05.960
So this is the
multicluster ingress,

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:07.745
or the north-south problem.

00:04:07.745 --> 00:04:09.620
And then when we bring
location into the mix,

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:11.420
things get a little
bit more complicated.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.850
Here we have service running
on two different regions.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:17.630
Let's say region 1 is in
Europe and region 2 is in Asia.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:20.240
So the users that are in
Europe or closer to region 1,

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.610
we want to deliver their
traffic to an instance

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.130
of a service that's closer
to them, so in Europe.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:27.650
And likewise, for the
users that are in Asia,

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:30.380
we want to deliver their traffic
to an instance of a service

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:31.610
that's running in Asia.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.920
Only when something
goes wrong, for example,

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:37.820
let's say everything in
the Europe region is down,

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.490
now we want to send the
traffic from European users

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:41.980
over to the Asia user.

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.623
So how do we manage all of that?

00:04:44.623 --> 00:04:45.790
And then, lastly, telemetry.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.415
Again, in a single
cluster environment,

00:04:47.415 --> 00:04:49.450
we have implementations
and tools

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:51.460
where we can measure
logging, and tracing,

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:53.230
and metrics for
every single service.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:55.090
How do we do that in
a distributed service?

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.405
Which signals to measure?

00:04:56.405 --> 00:04:58.030
How do we know if a
service is healthy?

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.108
What to alert on?

00:04:59.108 --> 00:05:01.150
So all of those things
we're going to talk about,

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.275
and we're going to use
Anthos Service Mesh to solve

00:05:03.275 --> 00:05:04.640
some of these things.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.430
So let's go to the demo.

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:08.200
Let's get oriented
to the environment.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.260
Here I have a Google Cloud
project with four GKE clusters

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.010
running.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:15.970
GKE 1 and 2 are running
in region US West 2.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.300
GKE 3 and 4 are running
in region US Central 1.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.185
So it's the same four-cluster
model that we just saw.

00:05:22.185 --> 00:05:23.560
Let's look at the
Workloads page.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.460
I've deployed a few
sample applications.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.300
And one of them is the
famous online boutique

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.707
retail application that
consists of 13 services.

00:05:31.707 --> 00:05:33.790
I've deployed each service
in their own namespace,

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:36.340
and you can see by the workloads
that each service is deployed

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:37.390
as a distributed service.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.610
So ad is running on
all four clusters.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.770
Cart is running on
all four clusters.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:44.290
Shopfrontend, which is how
you access the application,

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:46.472
is also running on
all four clusters.

00:05:46.472 --> 00:05:48.680
Let's take a closer look at
one of these deployments.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.610
So we're going to look at
shopfrontend running in GKE 1.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:53.380
And I'm going to
look at just the pods

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.743
for that particular one.

00:05:55.743 --> 00:05:57.910
You can see that this pod
is running two containers.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:05:59.680
One is the
shopfrontend container.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.200
That's your actual
application container.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.540
And the other one is
the asm sidecar proxy

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:06.400
called the istio-proxy.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.920
Now, this proxy governs
the flow of traffic

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.457
to and from this
particular container.

00:06:11.457 --> 00:06:13.540
And if we look at the
configuration of this proxy,

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.860
we can see what version
of asm we're running.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:18.430
So in this case,
we're running 1.10.4,

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.890
which also correlates
to the Istio version.

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:23.725
We can also see that we're
using the Google-managed meshca

00:06:23.725 --> 00:06:25.720
as our certificate authority.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:28.240
We can also see that our
trust domain is the workload

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:29.650
identity for the project.

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:32.350
And I'm using the same trust
domain for all four clusters.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.510
And that's how services
can talk and trust

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.670
each other across clusters.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:40.270
What we want to do is we want to
look at from this istio-proxy's

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:43.270
perspective, how does
it view other services?

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:45.220
Let's quickly jump
into the terminal.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:49.150
And I'm going to go ahead
into my GKE 1 cluster.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.140
Let's look at the pods.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.527
So kubectl get pods.

00:06:51.527 --> 00:06:53.110
This is the same pod
that we were just

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.240
looking at in the console.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:58.390
We're going to use the
istioctl CLI command.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:00.520
And I just want to look
at the proxy-config.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.770
And I just want to look at
the endpoints configuration

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.890
in the proxy-config for
this particular pod.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:07.997
And instead of dumping
out the entire config,

00:07:07.997 --> 00:07:10.330
I'm just going to grep it for
one of the services, which

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.313
is the cart service.

00:07:12.313 --> 00:07:14.230
And here you can see
that from the perspective

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:15.820
of this shopfrontend
pod, it sees

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:17.770
that there are four
endpoints out there

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:22.780
that it can access when it
accesses the cart service.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.730
These IP addresses
point to the pod

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:26.710
IP addresses of
the four endpoints.

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:28.323
Let's look at the pods.

00:07:28.323 --> 00:07:29.740
So the first thing
I'm going to do

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:35.200
is look at the pods
in the GKE 1 cluster

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.270
that's running in
shop-cart namespace.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:40.070
So shop-cart namespace is where
the cart service is running.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:41.620
And you can see that
in GKE 1 cluster

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:46.150
I only have one pod running with
the IP address of 10.0.2.11,

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:48.890
which is the same IP address
that you see up here,

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:52.210
which means that the other
three IP addresses correspond

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:54.197
to pods running in the
other three clusters.

00:07:54.197 --> 00:07:55.780
And we can quickly
verify that by just

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:58.840
picking one other cluster,
for example, GKE 4.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:01.210
And again, in GKE 4,
we're also just running

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:05.830
one cart pod with an IP
address of 10.8.2.143,

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:08.260
which is the IP address here.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:11.340
So the asm control plane goes
out and discovers the endpoints

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.740
for every single
service and configures

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:16.930
every single istio-proxy
with those endpoints.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:20.070
And that's how every single
service and every single pod

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.650
knows how to get to
every other service.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.050
So that's how the service
discovery piece is working.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.480
But we also want to make
sure that the traffic routing

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:28.680
east-west is working.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:32.250
So for example, the
front end service

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.360
can talk to the cart service.

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:36.630
So in order to demo
that, it's best done

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:38.470
visually through a dashboard.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:43.360
So here I have created a custom
dashboard for the cart service.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:46.020
And this dashboard here
on the left-hand side,

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.030
I'm looking at
the request rates.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:50.650
And I've grouped
them by location.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.220
So I want to see who
is accessing the cart

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.640
service from which location.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:59.220
And from the legend
here, you can

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:04.130
see that I'm receiving
requests to all four pods.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:06.860
Further, I can group this by,
let's say, a source service,

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:10.520
source_canonical_service.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.950
And here you can see that
there are only two services

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:14.570
accessing shop cart.

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:15.917
One is shopfrontend.

00:09:15.917 --> 00:09:17.000
The other one is checkout.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.150
And both services are
accessing all four locations.

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:23.300
And you can see that I have
a load generator that's

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.310
running that's showing me that
the traffic routing is working.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:29.510
So in GKE, every pod can
talk to every other pod

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:32.960
if you have deployed
GKE in VPC native mode

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.090
as long as you have the
appropriate firewall rules.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.630
So now east-west
traffic is working.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.150
Next, let's take a look
at north-south traffic

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:42.870
and how ingress works.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.560
So for that, let's go
back to our Clusters page.

00:09:48.038 --> 00:09:50.330
And you'll notice that I have
this fifth cluster called

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.080
the ingress-config cluster.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:53.960
This is the cluster
on how I configured

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.580
multicluster ingress.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.780
Let's take a closer
look at this cluster.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.630
So I'm going to go
back into my terminal.

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:02.720
I'm going to change my context
to the ingress-config cluster

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:05.570
and change my namespace
to istio-system.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:07.580
Inside of the
istio-system namespace,

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:08.900
I have two resources.

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:11.810
I have a multicluster
ingress resource,

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:14.450
and I also have a
multicluster service resource.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:15.920
And I have multiple
applications.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:18.820
And that's why you
see a couple of these.

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:21.990
So the multicluster ingress
resource corresponds

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.800
to an ingress resource and a
multicluster service resource

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:27.337
corresponds to a
service resource,

00:10:27.337 --> 00:10:28.920
just like you would
normally configure

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.535
an ingress and a service.

00:10:30.535 --> 00:10:32.410
So let's take a closer
look at each of these.

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:35.700
So I'm going to look at the
multicluster ingress resource

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:37.440
first for the online boutique.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:39.450
And you can see that
a multicluster ingress

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:41.680
resource looks a lot
like an ingress resource.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:43.500
So I have a rule with a host.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.330
So here's the host
of how I'm going

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:46.890
to access this application.

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:48.800
And its points back
to an app, a service.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:50.550
But in this case,
instead of pointing back

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:53.490
to a Kubernetes service, it's
pointing back to a multicluster

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.880
service on port 80.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:58.400
And if we look at the
multicluster service

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:01.230
resource that
corresponds to this,

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.200
again, it looks a lot like a
Kubernetes service resource.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:05.810
So if we look at
especially the spec

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:07.310
of that particular
resource, you can

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:10.640
see that it has a
service name, a port,

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:12.770
and then most importantly,
it has this selector.

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:16.100
And what it's selecting is
the istio-ingressgateway

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:17.750
as the backend service.

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:20.120
The only difference here
is these cluster selectors.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:21.495
And in this
particular case, I've

00:11:21.495 --> 00:11:23.098
selected all four clusters.

00:11:23.098 --> 00:11:25.640
So essentially what I've created
is the multicluster ingress.

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:28.520
You can think of it as the
front end that points back

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:31.430
to four different
backend services that

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:33.860
matches this selector, which
is the istio-ingressgateway

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:35.150
selector.

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:37.683
And what that creates, if
we go back to the console,

00:11:37.683 --> 00:11:39.350
and I'm going to go
to Network Services,

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:43.460
Load Balancing is a front
end with four back ends.

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:45.427
So here if we just
look at these top two,

00:11:45.427 --> 00:11:47.510
as a matter of fact, if
we look at the second load

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:50.690
balancer, which is the online
boutique, istio-mci, that got

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:53.720
created when we created
that MCI resource.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:55.590
And it gave me a front end.

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.710
We'll talk about the
certificate here shortly.

00:11:57.710 --> 00:12:00.830
But more importantly, we
have created four back ends.

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:02.540
And these four back
ends are pointing

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:05.030
to the istio-ingressgateway
service that's

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:06.263
running on all four clusters.

00:12:06.263 --> 00:12:08.180
And we can verify that
by looking at the zone.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:10.550
And you can see that all
four zones are covered.

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:12.650
And if we click on any
of these endpoints,

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:15.620
you can see the actual
pod IP addresses,

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:17.650
and then they are healthy.

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:20.780
If we go back to
this, the certificate,

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:24.262
I've created a Google managed
certificate for a DNS name.

00:12:24.262 --> 00:12:25.970
So remember, in the
multicluster ingress,

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:28.760
we had a host field that
pointed to this DNS name.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:30.830
And so in order to
access this service,

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:35.600
I can just simply copy this,
open a new tab, paste it,

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:37.850
and you can see that we can
access our online boutique

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:38.450
application.

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:40.440
And we can navigate
around this application.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.300
So here I'm looking at
product description,

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:43.580
adding it to the cart.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:45.620
Here I'm looking at
the cart service.

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:48.230
I can place the order, which
invokes the checkout service.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:49.760
And you can see
all the functions

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.790
are working for that
particular application.

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:55.730
The way the actual
traffic flow is working

00:12:55.730 --> 00:13:00.170
is when I access this
URL, if I scroll back,

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:02.960
I'm going to this
frontend IP address.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:06.440
From this frontend, traffic
is being routed to one or more

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.500
of these istio-ingressgateway
backends.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:11.840
And then the
istio-ingressgateway backend

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:14.820
routes the traffic
to the shopfrontend.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:18.190
So the next thing is the
implication of locations.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:20.400
So remember I have
istio-ingressgateway

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:22.950
running in two different regions
in four different clusters.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:24.840
I also have the
shopfrontend running

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:27.220
in both of these regions
in all four clusters.

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:30.750
So what I want is if I'm
closer to US West 2 region,

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:33.480
I want to go to the US West
2 istio-ingressgateway,

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:37.860
and then eventually also to
the US West 2 shopfrontends.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:42.080
So let's take a look at
how that is configured.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:44.630
I'm going to go back into
Kubernetes workloads.

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:47.060
And I have a load
generator running

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:48.650
for the online boutique.

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:50.930
And you notice that my
load generator is only

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:53.180
running in the West 2 region.

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:57.380
And this is so I can show
you locality load balancing.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:59.930
I also have locality load
balancing configured, but only

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:02.450
for the shopfrontend
application,

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:05.670
and the reason is just
for this demo purposes.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:08.180
So let's go to the console.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:11.280
Let's go to one of the
clusters, GKE 1, for example.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:13.490
And I'm going to show you
the destination rule that's

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:15.733
running for the shopfrontend.

00:14:15.733 --> 00:14:17.900
You can configure locality
load balancing mesh wide,

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:20.580
but I prefer to configure
it on a per service level.

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:23.000
So here I've configured it
just for the shopfrontend.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:24.770
And the destination
rule determines

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:27.510
how the traffic is routed once
the routing decision is made.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:29.900
And this is where you configure
locality load balancing.

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:31.580
So here in the load
balancer section

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:34.580
you can see that I have
locality load balancing enabled.

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:38.180
I also have a failover setting
which says go from US West

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:41.690
2 region to US Central 1,
since I'm looking at the GKE 1

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:42.917
configuration.

00:14:42.917 --> 00:14:44.750
And then I also have
this outlier detection,

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:48.120
which determines when a
failover or a failure occurs.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:51.140
This is a very aggressive rule
just for the sake of the demo.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:53.180
Essentially, I'm
saying in every second,

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:56.570
even if I have a single error,
consider that an outage,

00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:59.743
and eject that particular
deployment for a minute.

00:14:59.743 --> 00:15:01.660
And so you can see that
just with the addition

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:05.470
of these 5-10 lines, I've
created locality load

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:06.190
balancing.

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:07.990
And I have the same
exact destination

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:11.950
rules for all four clusters
for the frontend service.

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:14.130
So in order to prove that
that is actually working,

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:17.880
remember that my load generator
is only running in US West 2.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:22.110
So what I should expect
is only the West frontend

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:25.350
to be receiving traffic, because
nobody else is really accessing

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:27.370
this particular application.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:31.000
So the best way to do that
is let's look at the charts.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:33.060
So here I have a
custom dashboard

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:35.310
that I've created for
shopfrontend very similar

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:38.160
to the one that we were
looking at for cart earlier.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:40.590
Again, we're looking
at the request rates.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:42.850
And I've grouped
those by locations.

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:45.340
So I have the four locations.

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:47.940
So US West 2 a, b,
and Central 1 a, b.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:50.670
But you can see that I'm
only receiving traffic

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:53.490
to the shopfrontend only
in the West regions,

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:56.040
and not in the Central regions,
even though I have pods

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:57.840
running in the Central region.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:59.940
That means locality load
balancing is working.

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:02.190
And you can configure the
same locality load balancing

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:04.140
for every single service.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:07.200
So in a sunny day
scenario, that works great.

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:10.110
Everything will go to
its closest endpoint.

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:12.760
But what happens when
there is a failure?

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:17.250
So my load generator is
running in the West region.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:19.080
What happens if the
West region is down?

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:20.692
I should expect
that load generator

00:16:20.692 --> 00:16:22.650
to start sending traffic
to the Central region,

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:25.782
because I have configured
the failover rule as such.

00:16:25.782 --> 00:16:26.490
So let's do that.

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:28.800
In order to simulate
that, all I'm going to do

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:33.000
is scale the replicas for the
shopfrontend in the West region

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:34.760
down to 0.

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:37.370
So let's go to the console.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:41.310
And I'm going to run
the scale command.

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:43.270
And I'm just going to
fix this spelling error.

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:46.110
So all I'm doing is scaling
the shopfrontend deployment

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:53.410
replicas down to 0 in GKE
1, as well as in GKE 2.

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:56.320
These are my two
US West locations.

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:00.790
What that's going to do is
render a failover from US West

00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:02.620
2 region to US Central 1.

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:08.109
So we'll wait a few minutes
and see how the charts do.

00:17:08.109 --> 00:17:09.790
A few minutes have
passed, and now you

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:11.530
can see that all
of the traffic is

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:13.880
routed to the central servers.

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:18.280
So I have not changed the
load generator's location.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.890
It's still running in US West.

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:23.260
But because of the
failover from US West,

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:25.300
we're now going to Central 1.

00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:27.579
The chart to the right is
also showing us error rates

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:28.270
by location.

00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:29.740
You can see that
for the same time

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:33.410
that the failover occurred, we
did not experience any errors.

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:35.980
So it was a seamless failover.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:37.810
And we expect this
to kind of go back

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:40.000
when we change the
replicas back to one.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.410
So let's quickly do that.

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:45.100
So we go back to the console.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.690
All I'm going to do
is rerun that command.

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:53.740
But this time change the replica
to 1 for GKE 2 and for GKE 1,

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:56.270
we'll wait a few minutes.

00:17:56.270 --> 00:18:00.400
And now we see that the traffic
has gone back to the US West 2

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:03.860
clusters, and no more traffic
going to the central clusters.

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:07.240
So we're back to our sunny
day state of locality load

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:08.030
balancing.

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:12.070
And again, we did not
experience any errors

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:14.530
throughout this changeover.

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:16.420
So this technique is
also useful if you

00:18:16.420 --> 00:18:18.760
want to perform some scheduled
maintenance on a cluster.

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:21.010
You can now manually
and explicitly

00:18:21.010 --> 00:18:24.520
drain clusters by scaling
just the replicas down

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:27.610
for any cluster down to
zero, and shifting traffic

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:30.580
over to different clusters as
long as there's capacity there.

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:32.260
And then you can
perform an upgrade

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:36.530
or any kind of maintenance
on those clusters.

00:18:36.530 --> 00:18:38.030
The last thing we
want to look at

00:18:38.030 --> 00:18:41.490
is observability and
manageability of services.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:44.210
So for that, let's go
back to the Cloud Console.

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:49.170
And here we're going to
go to Anthos Service Mesh.

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:50.940
When you have Anthos
Service Mesh deployed

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:54.750
on one or more clusters, you
will get this dashboard view.

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:57.150
And this dashboard view
gives you a table view

00:18:57.150 --> 00:19:01.440
and a topology view for all
the Anthos enabled services.

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:03.420
So here I have
all of my services

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:05.550
that are running in
multiple clusters,

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:08.460
for example, shopfrontend
is running on four clusters.

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:11.580
I can look at which four
clusters it's running on.

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:13.170
This comes completely
out of the box

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:16.110
without you having to instrument
anything in the application.

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:18.900
If I click on shopfrontend,
I can get further information

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:19.750
about this service.

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:23.365
So I can see inbound
and outbound services

00:19:23.365 --> 00:19:24.240
that it's talking to.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.160
I can get the golden signals.

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:29.490
I can look at the metrics
for this particular service.

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:31.170
These are canned
dashboards, again,

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:33.308
that come with every
single service.

00:19:33.308 --> 00:19:35.100
So you get these
dashboards out of the box.

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:38.220
So you get requests per
second, error rates, latency

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.080
for the three
different percentiles,

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:44.130
the 50th, 95th, and 99th, as
well as request size, response

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:47.070
size, CPU, memory, and disk
for every single service

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:49.592
without having to
instrument anything.

00:19:49.592 --> 00:19:51.550
From here, you can also
go to Application Logs.

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:53.280
So I can just click
on Application Logs,

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:56.260
and it will create the
right filters for me.

00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:58.020
So I can just look at
the application logs

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:00.330
for the frontend
so I can see that I

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:02.830
have requests going in and out.

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:04.510
I can also look at
traces from here.

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:05.760
So I can just click on traces.

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:08.010
So if I want to look
at request trace,

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:12.400
this is something you do have to
configure in your application.

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:17.800
I can look at the histogram
of my requests coming in.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:20.560
And then I can click on
an individual request.

00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:23.470
And I can obviously look at that
particular trace information.

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:27.620
Again, all of this is part
of the info service mesh.

00:20:27.620 --> 00:20:30.470
Furthermore, I can go to
health, and I can create SLOs.

00:20:30.470 --> 00:20:33.770
So here I've created
a latency-based SLO

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:37.240
for the frontend service.

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:40.770
And if I open this up,
and if I edit this SLO,

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:43.120
I can just kind of show
you how I created it.

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:45.630
So the first thing is I
picked latency as my metric.

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:48.330
I can either choose
availability or latency.

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:50.190
I can hit Continue.

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:53.320
Here's the actual signal or
the metric that I'm using,

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:54.960
which is the response latencies.

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:56.940
I can see the historical
response latency.

00:20:56.940 --> 00:20:58.840
And based on that, I
just picked a value.

00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:02.280
So here I could see that there
are some spikes above 1,500

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:02.920
milliseconds.

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:06.203
So I picked one for
1,500 milliseconds.

00:21:06.203 --> 00:21:07.620
You give it the
compliance period.

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:09.090
So I'm picking calendar day.

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:11.220
And I'm keeping my goal at 95%.

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:13.530
So 95% of the time
I want my latency

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:15.720
to be 1,500
milliseconds or below

00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:17.250
for that particular service.

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:19.980
If I hit Continue, it also
gives me the JSON view

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:21.280
of that particular SLO.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:24.880
So I can use the SLO API
to create this if I'd like.

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:27.600
And then I just create my SLO.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:29.552
So for every single
service you can do that.

00:21:29.552 --> 00:21:31.760
And I recommend you do that
for every single service.

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:34.400
And you can do that as part
of your CI/CD pipeline.

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:37.560
This is where you'll get your
SLIs and your error budget,

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:39.710
as well as if you've
configured any alerts.

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:44.100
You can create alerts by
just clicking on this.

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:47.670
And you can measure how
exactly your service is being

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:50.050
experienced across the board.

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:51.720
So we typically
recommend at least

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:54.720
creating two SLOs for
every single service,

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:57.273
one for availability
and one for latency.

00:21:57.273 --> 00:21:59.190
So you can see whether
your users are actually

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:00.833
able to access your
service, and then

00:22:00.833 --> 00:22:02.250
what is their
experience when they

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:04.950
access that particular service.

00:22:04.950 --> 00:22:06.200
So that's the end of the demo.

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:08.850
Let's go back to
the presentation.

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:10.170
A few calls to action.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:12.000
First, hopefully
it's very obvious,

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:13.650
run distributed services.

00:22:13.650 --> 00:22:16.290
Never bet your services
on your infrastructure.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.380
By running your service on more
than one Kubernetes cluster,

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:21.300
you've immediately
gained the advantage

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:23.070
of scale and resiliency.

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:25.920
Second, monitor your
distributed services.

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:28.680
Golden signals is a
great place to start.

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:31.680
Somebody gave me this analogy
that when you go to the doctor,

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:34.590
they don't try to diagnose
you for every single disease.

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:36.780
They look at your symptoms,
and then they figure out

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:38.220
exactly what's wrong with you.

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:41.882
So alert on symptoms, not on
every single potential cause.

00:22:41.882 --> 00:22:44.340
In the distributed world, there
are thousands and thousands

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:45.215
of signals out there.

00:22:45.215 --> 00:22:47.160
And if you want to alert
on every single time

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:50.420
a CPU or memory spikes, you're
going to get overwhelmed.

00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:52.460
Lastly, use location
as a benefit.

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:54.830
I see two main
benefits for location,

00:22:54.830 --> 00:22:57.050
especially if you're running
multiregional services.

00:22:57.050 --> 00:23:01.340
First is you can deliver your
service closer to your user.

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:03.690
And secondly, thinking
about failure domains.

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:06.440
So I think of failure domains
in terms of concentric circles.

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:08.870
For instance, a Kubernetes
cluster is a failure domain.

00:23:08.870 --> 00:23:10.100
When a cluster
goes down, it only

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:11.767
takes down the services
that are running

00:23:11.767 --> 00:23:14.060
on that cluster A zone
is a failure domain.

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:16.940
If all the clusters in a zone
or the zone itself goes down,

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:18.650
then that zone
cannot serve traffic.

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:20.180
And a region is
a failure domain.

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.010
Thinking in those
terms helps you

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:24.680
kind of do figure out how
to do maintenance windows

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:27.170
and scheduled operations
on your infrastructure.

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:29.480
If you want to build
distributed services,

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:34.580
you can go to this link,
bit.ly/run-distributed-services,

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:37.100
or you can pause the video and
scan this QR code that takes

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:38.387
you to the same link.

00:23:38.387 --> 00:23:39.470
I thank you for your time.

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:42.460
And enjoy the rest
of the conference.

